<PageLayout
  title="How we test"
  description="Our review methodology — what counts as a tested tool, how we score, what we re-test, and what we won't accept money for."
  eyebrow="Methodology"
  ogSlug="methodology"
>
  <p>
    This page exists so that you can hold us accountable. If we ever publish a review that doesn't live up to what's described below, <a href="/contact/">tell us</a> and we will fix it or pull it.
  </p>

  <h2>What counts as "tested"</h2>

  <p>
    Every product we review meets one of two thresholds:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Production use.</strong> The software has been used in real work, by at least one member of our team, for at least one full pricing cycle (typically 30 days). For team-collaboration tools, the threshold is 30 days with at least three team members.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Structured trial.</strong> When production use is impractical, we run a 30-day structured trial against a published checklist for that category. The checklist lives at the bottom of each roundup. The trial uses a representative use case, not an idealized one.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Whichever threshold applies, the <code>last tested</code> date on the review is the last day we used the software in earnest. If it's older than nine months, we re-test before re-promoting.
  </p>

  <h2>How we score</h2>

  <p>
    Ratings are out of 5.0, in increments of 0.1. They are a holistic judgment, not an average across sub-scores. A 4.0 means we'd recommend the tool to a friend in the right context. A 5.0 means we'd recommend it to a friend in any context — there is currently nothing on the site at 5.0.
  </p>

  <p>
    We do not publish "scores out of 100." Software is not benchmarked, it is judged. A high score is a recommendation, not a measurement.
  </p>

  <h2>What we won't accept money for</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Reviewing a product (we review what we think is worth reviewing).</li>
    <li>A higher score on an existing review.</li>
    <li>Including a product in a roundup.</li>
    <li>Removing a competitor from a roundup.</li>
    <li>Letting a vendor review a piece before it publishes. We may share a fact-check excerpt; we don't share the whole piece.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    We accept affiliate commissions, which are paid by the vendor when a reader buys after clicking through. The commission rate has no influence on scoring. The same affiliate program might pay us 30% on one tool and 5% on another; the score is what it is.
  </p>

  <h2>How we handle corrections</h2>

  <p>
    When we get something wrong — a feature mis-described, a price out of date, a product changed substantially — we update the piece and add a dated correction note at the bottom. We do not silently rewrite published reviews.
  </p>

  <h2>Conflicts of interest</h2>

  <p>
    If an author has a personal investment in, advisory role with, or significant financial relationship to a vendor, the author does not write the review. If the relationship is incidental (e.g., a former employer), we disclose it inline. If you spot a conflict we missed, <a href="/contact/">tell us</a>.
  </p>
</PageLayout>
